    question: "What is the 'Write Once, Run Anywhere' principle?",
    idealAnswer: "**WORA Principle**: Java code compiled to bytecode can run on any platform with a compatible JVM.\n\n**The WORA Process**:\n\n**1. Write Java Source Code**:\n```java\n// HelloWorld.java - Write once\npublic class HelloWorld {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"Hello from Java!\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**2. Compile to Bytecode**:\n```bash\n# Compile on any platform (Windows, Mac, Linux)\njavac HelloWorld.java\n# Creates: HelloWorld.class (platform-independent bytecode)\n```\n\n**3. Run Anywhere**:\n```bash\n# Same .class file runs on any platform with JVM\njava HelloWorld\n# Output: Hello from Java!\n```\n\n**4. Bytecode Analysis**:\n```java\n// Bytecode is platform-independent\n// JVM translates bytecode to native machine code\njavap -c HelloWorld.class  // View bytecode instructions\n\n// Example bytecode structure:\n// 0: getstatic     #2                  // Field java/lang/System.out:Ljava/io/PrintStream;\n// 3: ldc           #3                  // String Hello from Java!\n// 5: invokevirtual #4                  // Method java/io/PrintStream.println:(Ljava/lang/String;)V\n// 8: return\n```\n\n**5. Platform-Specific JVMs**:\n```java\n// Different JVM implementations for each platform:\n// - Windows JVM: Translates bytecode to Windows machine code\n// - macOS JVM: Translates bytecode to macOS machine code\n// - Linux JVM: Translates bytecode to Linux machine code\n// - All produce same output for same bytecode\n```\n\n**6. Real-World Benefits**:\n```java\n// Enterprise application example\npublic class BankingApp {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"Banking System v1.0\");\n        // Same application runs on:\n        // - Windows servers (bank branches)\n        // - Linux servers (data centers)\n        // - macOS servers (development)\n        // - Mobile devices (via Android JVM)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Key Components**:\n- **Bytecode**: Intermediate, platform-independent code\n- **JVM**: Platform-specific runtime environment\n- **Class Loader**: Loads bytecode into memory\n- **JIT Compiler**: Optimizes bytecode at runtime\n\n**Limitations**:\n- Platform-specific features (file paths, UI) need abstraction\n- Performance varies between JVM implementations\n- Some native libraries require platform-specific versions",
