    question: "What are the primitive data types in Java?",
    idealAnswer: "**8 Primitive Data Types**: Direct values stored in memory, not objects.\n\n**1. Integer Types**:\n```java\npublic class PrimitiveTypes {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // byte: 8-bit (-128 to 127)\n        byte age = 25;\n        byte temperature = -10;\n        \n        // short: 16-bit (-32,768 to 32,767)\n        short year = 2024;\n        short population = 50000;\n        \n        // int: 32-bit (-2^31 to 2^31-1)\n        int salary = 75000;\n        int distance = 1000000;\n        \n        // long: 64-bit (-2^63 to 2^63-1)\n        long worldPopulation = 8000000000L;  // Note: L suffix\n        long milliseconds = System.currentTimeMillis();\n        \n        System.out.println(\"Byte: \" + age);\n        System.out.println(\"Short: \" + year);\n        System.out.println(\"Int: \" + salary);\n        System.out.println(\"Long: \" + worldPopulation);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**2. Floating-Point Types**:\n```java\n// float: 32-bit (6-7 decimal digits)\nfloat pi = 3.14f;           // Note: f suffix\nfloat temperature = 98.6f;\nfloat price = 19.99f;\n\n// double: 64-bit (15-16 decimal digits)\ndouble precisePi = 3.141592653589793;\ndouble bankBalance = 1234567.89;\ndouble scientificNotation = 1.23e-4;  // 0.000123\n\nSystem.out.println(\"Float: \" + pi);\nSystem.out.println(\"Double: \" + precisePi);\n```\n\n**3. Boolean Type**:\n```java\nboolean isLoggedIn = true;\nboolean hasPermission = false;\nboolean isComplete = (5 > 3);  // Results in true\n\nif (isLoggedIn && hasPermission) {\n    System.out.println(\"Access granted\");\n}\n```\n\n**4. Character Type**:\n```java\n// char: 16-bit Unicode (0 to 65,535)\nchar grade = 'A';\nchar currency = '$';\nchar emoji = '😀';  // Unicode emoji\nchar unicodeChar = '\\u03A9';  // Greek letter Omega (Ω)\n\nSystem.out.println(\"Grade: \" + grade);\nSystem.out.println(\"Unicode: \" + unicodeChar);\n```\n\n**5. Default Values**:\n```java\npublic class DefaultValueExample {\n    // Instance variables get default values\n    byte defaultByte;        // 0\n    short defaultShort;      // 0\n    int defaultInt;          // 0\n    long defaultLong;        // 0L\n    float defaultFloat;      // 0.0f\n    double defaultDouble;    // 0.0d\n    boolean defaultBoolean;  // false\n    char defaultChar;        // '\\u0000' (null character)\n    \n    // Local variables must be initialized before use\n    public void demonstrate() {\n        int localVar = 10;  // Must initialize\n        System.out.println(\"Local: \" + localVar);\n        System.out.println(\"Default int: \" + defaultInt);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**6. Type Conversion Examples**:\n```java\n// Widening conversion (automatic)\nint intValue = 100;\nlong longValue = intValue;    // int to long\nfloat floatValue = intValue;   // int to float\n\nt// Narrowing conversion (requires casting)\ndouble doubleValue = 99.99;\nint intValueFromDouble = (int) doubleValue;  // Results in 99 (truncates)\n\n// Character to int conversion\nchar letter = 'A';\nint asciiValue = letter;  // 65\n```\n\n**Memory Usage**:\n- byte: 1 byte\n- short: 2 bytes\n- int: 4 bytes\n- long: 8 bytes\n- float: 4 bytes\n- double: 8 bytes\n- boolean: 1 byte (typically)\n- char: 2 bytes",
