    id: 6,
    category: "Java Basics",
    question: "What is type casting in Java?",
    idealAnswer: "**Type Casting**: Converting one data type to another.\n\n**1. Widening Type Casting (Automatic)**:\n```java\npublic class WideningExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Automatic conversion from smaller to larger types\n        byte b = 10;\n        short s = b;        // byte → short (automatic)\n        int i = s;          // short → int (automatic)\n        long l = i;         // int → long (automatic)\n        float f = l;        // long → float (automatic)\n        double d = f;       // float → double (automatic)\n        \n        System.out.println(\"byte: \" + b);     // 10\n        System.out.println(\"double: \" + d);   // 10.0\n        \n        // Widening in expressions\n        int x = 5;\n        double y = 2.5;\n        double result = x * y;  // int promoted to double automatically\n        System.out.println(\"Result: \" + result);  // 12.5\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**2. Narrowing Type Casting (Manual)**:\n```java\npublic class NarrowingExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Manual conversion from larger to smaller types\n        double d = 99.99;\n        float f = (float) d;      // double → float (requires cast)\n        long l = (long) f;         // float → long (requires cast)\n        int i = (int) l;           // long → int (requires cast)\n        short s = (short) i;       // int → short (requires cast)\n        byte b = (byte) s;         // short → byte (requires cast)\n        \n        System.out.println(\"Original double: \" + d);  // 99.99\n        System.out.println(\"Final byte: \" + b);      // 99 (data loss!)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**3. Data Loss Examples**:\n```java\npublic class DataLossExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Floating point to integer (truncation)\n        double pi = 3.14159;\n        int intPi = (int) pi;      // Results in 3 (decimal part lost)\n        \n        // Large to small integer (overflow)\n        int largeInt = 300;\n        byte smallByte = (byte) largeInt;  // Results in 44 (overflow!)\n        \n        // Precision loss\n        double precise = 123456789.987654321;\n        float lessPrecise = (float) precise;  // Loses precision\n        \n        System.out.println(\"Double to int: \" + intPi);      // 3\n        System.out.println(\"Int to byte: \" + smallByte);    // 44\n        System.out.println(\"Precision loss: \" + lessPrecise); // 1.23456792E8\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**4. Reference Type Casting**:\n```java\nclass Animal {\n    void makeSound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal sound\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    void makeSound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Woof\");\n    }\n    \n    void fetch() {\n        System.out.println(\"Fetching ball\");\n    }\n}\n\npublic class ReferenceCasting {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Upcasting (automatic)\n        Dog dog = new Dog();\n        Animal animal = dog;  // Dog → Animal (automatic)\n        \n        // Downcasting (manual)\n        Animal genericAnimal = new Dog();\n        Dog specificDog = (Dog) genericAnimal;  // Animal → Dog (requires cast)\n        specificDog.fetch();  // Valid only after downcast\n        \n        // Invalid casting (ClassCastException at runtime)\n        Animal catAnimal = new Animal();\n        // Dog catDog = (Dog) catAnimal;  // RUNTIME ERROR!\n        \n        // Safe casting with instanceof\n        if (genericAnimal instanceof Dog) {\n            Dog safeDog = (Dog) genericAnimal;\n            safeDog.fetch();\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**5. Casting in Expressions**:\n```java\npublic class ExpressionCasting {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Prevent integer division\n        int a = 5, b = 2;\n        double result1 = a / b;           // 2.0 (integer division)\n        double result2 = (double) a / b;   // 2.5 (double division)\n        \n        // Cast entire expression\n        double result3 = (double) (a / b);  // 2.0 (integer division then cast)\n        \n        System.out.println(\"Integer division: \" + result1);  // 2.0\n        System.out.println(\"Double division: \" + result2);  // 2.5\n        \n        // Overflow prevention\n        int x = 1000000;\n        int y = 1000000;\n        // long product = x * y;           // Overflow! (-727379968)\n        long product = (long) x * y;       // Cast first, then multiply\n        System.out.println(\"Product: \" + product);  // 1000000000000\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**6. Type Conversion Rules**:\n```java\n// Conversion hierarchy (smallest to largest):\n// byte → short → int → long → float → double\n//                ↗ char ↗\n\n// Automatic promotions in mixed operations:\n// 1. All byte, short, char promoted to int\n// 2. If any operand is long, whole expression promoted to long\n// 3. If any operand is float, whole expression promoted to float\n// 4. If any operand is double, whole expression promoted to double\n```",
