    category: "Java Basics",
    question: "What is the difference between == and equals()?",
    idealAnswer: "**== Operator**: Compares references (memory addresses) for objects, values for primitives.\n**equals() Method**: Compares content/values of objects (can be overridden).\n\n**1. Primitive Types (== works as expected)**:\n```java\npublic class PrimitiveComparison {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int a = 5;\n        int b = 5;\n        int c = 10;\n        \n        System.out.println(\"a == b: \" + (a == b));  // true (same values)\n        System.out.println(\"a == c: \" + (a == c));  // false (different values)\n        \n        // equals() doesn't apply to primitives\n        // a.equals(b); // COMPILE ERROR!\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**2. String Comparison (Key Difference)**:\n```java\npublic class StringComparison {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // String literals (interned - same memory location)\n        String str1 = \"Hello\";\n        String str2 = \"Hello\";\n        \n        System.out.println(\"str1 == str2: \" + (str1 == str2));      // true (same reference)\n        System.out.println(\"str1.equals(str2): \" + str1.equals(str2)); // true (same content)\n        \n        // String objects (different memory locations)\n        String str3 = new String(\"Hello\");\n        String str4 = new String(\"Hello\");\n        \n        System.out.println(\"str3 == str4: \" + (str3 == str4));      // false (different references)\n        System.out.println(\"str3.equals(str4): \" + str3.equals(str4)); // true (same content)\n        \n        // Mixed comparison\n        System.out.println(\"str1 == str3: \" + (str1 == str3));      // false\n        System.out.println(\"str1.equals(str3): \" + str1.equals(str3)); // true\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**3. Custom Objects (Default equals() behavior)**:\n```java\nclass Person {\n    String name;\n    int age;\n    \n    Person(String name, int age) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.age = age;\n    }\n    \n    // No equals() override - uses default behavior\n}\n\npublic class DefaultEqualsExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Person p1 = new Person(\"Alice\", 25);\n        Person p2 = new Person(\"Alice\", 25);\n        Person p3 = p1;  // Same reference\n        \n        System.out.println(\"p1 == p2: \" + (p1 == p2));      // false (different objects)\n        System.out.println(\"p1.equals(p2): \" + p1.equals(p2)); // false (default equals() uses ==)\n        \n        System.out.println(\"p1 == p3: \" + (p1 == p3));      // true (same reference)\n        System.out.println(\"p1.equals(p3): \" + p1.equals(p3)); // true (same reference)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**4. Custom Objects with Overridden equals()**:\n```java\nclass Student {\n    String name;\n    int id;\n    \n    Student(String name, int id) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.id = id;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Object obj) {\n        // 1. Check if same reference\n        if (this == obj) return true;\n        \n        // 2. Check if null or different class\n        if (obj == null || getClass() != obj.getClass()) return false;\n        \n        // 3. Compare content\n        Student other = (Student) obj;\n        return id == other.id && name.equals(other.name);\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public int hashCode() {\n        return Objects.hash(name, id);  // Must override hashCode too!\n    }\n}\n\npublic class CustomEqualsExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Student s1 = new Student(\"Alice\", 1001);\n        Student s2 = new Student(\"Alice\", 1001);\n        Student s3 = new Student(\"Bob\", 1001);\n        \n        System.out.println(\"s1 == s2: \" + (s1 == s2));      // false (different objects)\n        System.out.println(\"s1.equals(s2): \" + s1.equals(s2)); // true (same content)\n        \n        System.out.println(\"s1.equals(s3): \" + s1.equals(s3)); // false (different names)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**5. String Interning**:\n```java\npublic class StringInterning {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String s1 = \"Java\";           // String literal (interned)\n        String s2 = new String(\"Java\"); // String object\n        String s3 = s2.intern();      // Intern the string\n        \n        System.out.println(\"s1 == s2: \" + (s1 == s2));      // false\n        System.out.println(\"s1 == s3: \" + (s1 == s3));      // true (both interned)\n        \n        // When to use intern():\n        // - When comparing strings frequently\n        // - When memory usage is critical\n        // - When you need reference equality for same content\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**6. Best Practices**:\n```java\npublic class BestPractices {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // ✅ Use equals() for object content comparison\n        String name1 = \"John\";\n        String name2 = \"John\";\n        if (name1.equals(name2)) {\n            System.out.println(\"Same name\");\n        }\n        \n        // ✅ Use == for primitive comparison\n        int x = 5, y = 5;\n        if (x == y) {\n            System.out.println(\"Same value\");\n        }\n        \n        // ✅ Use == for null check before equals()\n        String str = null;\n        if (str != null && str.equals(\"test\")) {\n            // Safe comparison\n        }\n        \n        // ✅ Or use Objects.equals() for null-safe comparison\n        import java.util.Objects;\n        if (Objects.equals(str, \"test\")) {\n            // Null-safe comparison\n        }\n        \n        // ❌ Avoid equals() on literals (NPE risk)\n        // if (\"test\".equals(str)) { }  // Better but still risky\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Key Rules**:\n- **==**: Reference comparison for objects, value comparison for primitives\n- **equals()**: Content comparison for objects (when properly overridden)\n- **Always override hashCode() when overriding equals()**\n- **Use Objects.equals() for null-safe comparisons**",
    difficulty: "Easy"
