    id: 18,
    category: "OOP Concepts",
    question: "What is encapsulation?",
    idealAnswer: "**Encapsulation**: Bundling data (fields) and methods that operate on data together within a class.\n\n**1. Basic Encapsulation Example**:\n```java\npublic class Student {\n    // Private fields (data hiding)\n    private String name;\n    private int age;\n    private double gpa;\n    \n    // Public constructor\n    public Student(String name, int age, double gpa) {\n        this.name = name;\n        setAge(age);  // Use setter for validation\n        setGpa(gpa);  // Use setter for validation\n    }\n    \n    // Public getters (controlled read access)\n    public String getName() {\n        return name;\n    }\n    \n    public int getAge() {\n        return age;\n    }\n    \n    public double getGpa() {\n        return gpa;\n    }\n    \n    // Public setters (controlled write access with validation)\n    public void setName(String name) {\n        if (name != null && !name.trim().isEmpty()) {\n            this.name = name.trim();\n        } else {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Name cannot be null or empty\");\n        }\n    }\n    \n    public void setAge(int age) {\n        if (age >= 0 && age <= 120) {\n            this.age = age;\n        } else {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Age must be between 0 and 120\");\n        }\n    }\n    \n    public void setGpa(double gpa) {\n        if (gpa >= 0.0 && gpa <= 4.0) {\n            this.gpa = gpa;\n        } else {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"GPA must be between 0.0 and 4.0\");\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // Business logic method\n    public boolean isHonorRoll() {\n        return gpa >= 3.5;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**2. Encapsulation vs No Encapsulation**:\n```java\n// WITHOUT ENCAPSULATION (Bad practice)\nclass BadStudent {\n    String name;  // Public - anyone can modify\n    int age;      // Public - no validation\n    double gpa;   // Public - no control\n}\n\n// WITH ENCAPSULATION (Good practice)\nclass GoodStudent {\n    private String name;  // Private - protected\n    private int age;      // Private - validated\n    private double gpa;   // Private - controlled\n    \n    // Public methods with validation\n    public void setAge(int age) {\n        if (age >= 0 && age <= 120) {\n            this.age = age;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\npublic class EncapsulationDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // BadStudent - no protection\n        BadStudent bad = new BadStudent();\n        bad.name = \"\";        // Invalid name allowed\n        bad.age = -5;         // Invalid age allowed\n        bad.gpa = 5.0;        // Invalid GPA allowed\n        \n        // GoodStudent - protected\n        GoodStudent good = new GoodStudent();\n        // good.name = \"\";     // COMPILE ERROR - private\n        // good.age = -5;      // COMPILE ERROR - private\n        good.setAge(-5);      // RUNTIME ERROR - validation\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**3. Advanced Encapsulation with Immutability**:\n```java\npublic final class ImmutablePerson {\n    // Final fields - cannot be changed after construction\n    private final String name;\n    private final int age;\n    private final List<String> hobbies;  // Defensive copy needed\n    \n    public ImmutablePerson(String name, int age, List<String> hobbies) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.age = age;\n        // Defensive copy to protect internal state\n        this.hobbies = new ArrayList<>(hobbies);\n    }\n    \n    // Only getters - no setters\n    public String getName() {\n        return name;\n    }\n    \n    public int getAge() {\n        return age;\n    }\n    \n    // Defensive copy for mutable objects\n    public List<String> getHobbies() {\n        return new ArrayList<>(hobbies);\n    }\n    \n    // Create new instance for modifications\n    public ImmutablePerson withAge(int newAge) {\n        return new ImmutablePerson(this.name, newAge, this.hobbies);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**4. Encapsulation in Real-World Scenario**:\n```java\npublic class BankAccount {\n    private double balance;\n    private String accountNumber;\n    private boolean isFrozen;\n    \n    public BankAccount(String accountNumber, double initialBalance) {\n        this.accountNumber = accountNumber;\n        this.balance = Math.max(0, initialBalance);  // Validation\n        this.isFrozen = false;\n    }\n    \n    // Controlled deposit with validation\n    public boolean deposit(double amount) {\n        if (amount <= 0) {\n            System.out.println(\"Deposit amount must be positive\");\n            return false;\n        }\n        \n        if (isFrozen) {\n            System.out.println(\"Account is frozen\");\n            return false;\n        }\n        \n        balance += amount;\n        System.out.println(\"Deposited: $\" + amount + \", New balance: $\" + balance);\n        return true;\n    }\n    \n    // Controlled withdrawal with validation\n    public boolean withdraw(double amount) {\n        if (amount <= 0) {\n            System.out.println(\"Withdrawal amount must be positive\");\n            return false;\n        }\n        \n        if (isFrozen) {\n            System.out.println(\"Account is frozen\");\n            return false;\n        }\n        \n        if (amount > balance) {\n            System.out.println(\"Insufficient funds\");\n            return false;\n        }\n        \n        balance -= amount;\n        System.out.println(\"Withdrew: $\" + amount + \", New balance: $\" + balance);\n        return true;\n    }\n    \n    // Read-only access\n    public double getBalance() {\n        return balance;\n    }\n    \n    // Internal method (private)\n    private void logTransaction(String type, double amount) {\n        // Log transaction details\n        System.out.println(\"Transaction: \" + type + \", Amount: $\" + amount);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**5. Benefits of Encapsulation**:\n```java\npublic class EncapsulationBenefits {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // 1. DATA PROTECTION\n        Student student = new Student(\"Alice\", 20, 3.8);\n        // student.age = -5;  // IMPOSSIBLE - data protected\n        \n        // 2. VALIDATION\n        try {\n            student.setAge(-5);  // VALIDATION CATCHES ERROR\n        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"Validation caught: \" + e.getMessage());\n        }\n        \n        // 3. FLEXIBILITY - can change implementation\n        // Internal storage can change without affecting users\n        \n        // 4. MAINTAINABILITY - easier to debug and modify\n        // All access goes through controlled methods\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Key Principles**:\n- **Private fields**: Protect data from direct access\n- **Public methods**: Provide controlled access\n- **Validation**: Ensure data integrity\n- **Immutability**: Create objects that can't be modified\n- **Defensive copying**: Protect mutable internal state",
    difficulty: "Easy"
