    question: "What is polymorphism?",
    idealAnswer: "**Polymorphism**: Ability of an object to take many forms. Same method name, different behaviors.\n\n**1. Compile-time Polymorphism (Method Overloading)**:\n```java\nclass Calculator {\n    // Method overloading - same name, different parameters\n    public int add(int a, int b) {\n        System.out.println(\"Adding two integers\");\n        return a + b;\n    }\n    \n    public double add(double a, double b) {\n        System.out.println(\"Adding two doubles\");\n        return a + b;\n    }\n    \n    public int add(int a, int b, int c) {\n        System.out.println(\"Adding three integers\");\n        return a + b + c;\n    }\n    \n    public String add(String a, String b) {\n        System.out.println(\"Concatenating strings\");\n        return a + b;\n    }\n}\n\npublic class OverloadingDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Calculator calc = new Calculator();\n        \n        // Compiler determines which method to call based on parameters\n        System.out.println(calc.add(5, 3));        // Calls int add(int, int)\n        System.out.println(calc.add(5.5, 3.2));    // Calls double add(double, double)\n        System.out.println(calc.add(1, 2, 3));     // Calls int add(int, int, int)\n        System.out.println(calc.add(\"Hello\", \"World\")); // Calls String add(String, String)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**2. Runtime Polymorphism (Method Overriding)**:\n```java\n// Parent class\nclass Animal {\n    public void makeSound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal makes a sound\");\n    }\n    \n    public void move() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal moves\");\n    }\n}\n\n// Child classes overriding parent methods\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    public void makeSound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog barks: Woof!\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void move() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog runs\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Cat extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    public void makeSound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Cat meows: Meow!\");\n    }\n    \n    // Inherit move() from parent\n}\n\nclass Bird extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    public void makeSound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Bird chirps: Tweet!\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void move() {\n        System.out.println(\"Bird flies\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**3. Runtime Polymorphism in Action**:\n```java\npublic class PolymorphismDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Same reference type, different object types\n        Animal animal1 = new Dog();\n        Animal animal2 = new Cat();\n        Animal animal3 = new Bird();\n        Animal animal4 = new Animal();\n        \n        // Runtime polymorphism - method determined at runtime\n        animal1.makeSound();  // Dog barks: Woof!\n        animal2.makeSound();  // Cat meows: Meow!\n        animal3.makeSound();  // Bird chirps: Tweet!\n        animal4.makeSound();  // Animal makes a sound\n        \n        animal1.move();      // Dog runs\n        animal2.move();      // Animal moves (inherited)\n        animal3.move();      // Bird flies\n        \n        // Polymorphic array\n        Animal[] animals = {\n            new Dog(), new Cat(), new Bird(), new Animal()\n        };\n        \n        System.out.println(\"\\nPolymorphic array:\");\n        for (Animal animal : animals) {\n            animal.makeSound();  // Each object calls its own version\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**4. Polymorphism with Method Parameters**:\n```java\nclass ShapeProcessor {\n    // Method that accepts any Animal subclass\n    public void processAnimal(Animal animal) {\n        System.out.println(\"Processing: \" + animal.getClass().getSimpleName());\n        animal.makeSound();  // Polymorphic call\n        animal.move();\n    }\n    \n    // Method that returns different animal types\n    public Animal createAnimal(String type) {\n        switch (type.toLowerCase()) {\n            case \"dog\":\n                return new Dog();\n            case \"cat\":\n                return new Cat();\n            case \"bird\":\n                return new Bird();\n            default:\n                return new Animal();\n        }\n    }\n}\n\npublic class ParameterPolymorphism {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        ShapeProcessor processor = new ShapeProcessor();\n        \n        // Same method, different object types\n        processor.processAnimal(new Dog());\n        processor.processAnimal(new Cat());\n        processor.processAnimal(new Bird());\n        \n        // Factory method returning different types\n        Animal myPet = processor.createAnimal(\"dog\");\n        myPet.makeSound();  // Dog barks: Woof!\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**5. Advanced Polymorphism with Interfaces**:\n```java\n// Interface for polymorphic behavior\ninterface Drawable {\n    void draw();\n    void resize(double factor);\n}\n\n// Multiple implementations of the same interface\nclass Circle implements Drawable {\n    private double radius;\n    \n    public Circle(double radius) {\n        this.radius = radius;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void draw() {\n        System.out.println(\"Drawing circle with radius \" + radius);\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void resize(double factor) {\n        radius *= factor;\n        System.out.println(\"Resized circle to radius \" + radius);\n    }\n}\n\nclass Rectangle implements Drawable {\n    private double width, height;\n    \n    public Rectangle(double width, double height) {\n        this.width = width;\n        this.height = height;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void draw() {\n        System.out.println(\"Drawing rectangle \" + width + \"x\" + height);\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void resize(double factor) {\n        width *= factor;\n        height *= factor;\n        System.out.println(\"Resized rectangle to \" + width + \"x\" + height);\n    }\n}\n\n// Polymorphic drawing system\nclass DrawingApp {\n    public void drawShape(Drawable shape) {\n        shape.draw();  // Polymorphic call\n    }\n    \n    public void resizeShape(Drawable shape, double factor) {\n        shape.resize(factor);  // Polymorphic call\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**6. Benefits of Polymorphism**:\n```java\npublic class PolymorphismBenefits {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // 1. FLEXIBILITY - Same code works with different types\n        Animal[] zoo = {new Dog(), new Cat(), new Bird()};\n        \n        // No need to know exact type\n        for (Animal animal : zoo) {\n            animal.makeSound();  // Works for any Animal subclass\n        }\n        \n        // 2. EXTENSIBILITY - Add new types without changing existing code\n        // New Fish class can be added without modifying this loop\n        \n        // 3. MAINTAINABILITY - Easier to modify and extend\n        // Changes to base class automatically affect subclasses\n        \n        // 4. CODE REUSE - Same method works for multiple types\n        processAnimal(new Dog());\n        processAnimal(new Cat());\n        processAnimal(new Bird());\n    }\n    \n    // Generic method that works with any Animal\n    public static void processAnimal(Animal animal) {\n        animal.makeSound();\n        animal.move();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Key Polymorphism Concepts**:\n- **Method Overloading**: Compile-time, same class, different parameters\n- **Method Overriding**: Runtime, parent-child, same signature\n- **Upcasting**: Child reference assigned to parent variable\n- **Dynamic Method Dispatch**: Runtime method selection\n- **Interface Polymorphism**: Multiple implementations of same interface",
