    category: "OOP Concepts",
    question: "What is method overloading?",
    idealAnswer: "**Method Overloading**: Multiple methods with same name but different parameters in same class.\n\n**1. Basic Method Overloading Examples**:\n```java\nclass Calculator {\n    // Overload by parameter types\n    public int add(int a, int b) {\n        System.out.println(\"Adding two integers\");\n        return a + b;\n    }\n    \n    public double add(double a, double b) {\n        System.out.println(\"Adding two doubles\");\n        return a + b;\n    }\n    \n    // Overload by parameter count\n    public int add(int a, int b, int c) {\n        System.out.println(\"Adding three integers\");\n        return a + b + c;\n    }\n    \n    // Overload by parameter order\n    public String display(String name, int age) {\n        return name + \" is \" + age + \" years old\";\n    }\n    \n    public String display(int age, String name) {\n        return \"Age \" + age + \", Name \" + name;\n    }\n    \n    // Overload with varargs (variable arguments)\n    public int sum(int... numbers) {\n        int total = 0;\n        for (int num : numbers) {\n            total += num;\n        }\n        return total;\n    }\n    \n    public double sum(double... numbers) {\n        double total = 0.0;\n        for (double num : numbers) {\n            total += num;\n        }\n        return total;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**2. Method Overloading Usage**:\n```java\npublic class OverloadingDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Calculator calc = new Calculator();\n        \n        // Different calls based on parameter types\n        System.out.println(calc.add(5, 3));        // int add(int, int)\n        System.out.println(calc.add(5.5, 3.2));    // double add(double, double)\n        System.out.println(calc.add(1, 2, 3));     // int add(int, int, int)\n        \n        // Different parameter order\n        System.out.println(calc.display(\"Alice\", 25));  // display(String, int)\n        System.out.println(calc.display(25, \"Alice\"));  // display(int, String)\n        \n        // Varargs usage\n        System.out.println(calc.sum(1, 2, 3, 4, 5));     // sum(int...)\n        System.out.println(calc.sum(1.1, 2.2, 3.3));     // sum(double...)\n        System.out.println(calc.sum());                   // sum(int...) with 0 args\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**3. Constructor Overloading**:\n```java\nclass Employee {\n    private String name;\n    private int id;\n    private double salary;\n    private String department;\n    \n    // Default constructor\n    public Employee() {\n        this(\"Unknown\", 0, 0.0, \"Unassigned\");\n    }\n    \n    // Constructor with name and id\n    public Employee(String name, int id) {\n        this(name, id, 30000.0, \"General\");\n    }\n    \n    // Constructor with name, id, and salary\n    public Employee(String name, int id, double salary) {\n        this(name, id, salary, \"General\");\n    }\n    \n    // Full constructor\n    public Employee(String name, int id, double salary, String department) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.id = id;\n        this.salary = salary;\n        this.department = department;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public String toString() {\n        return String.format(\"Employee{name='%s', id=%d, salary=%.2f, dept='%s'}\", \n                           name, id, salary, department);\n    }\n}\n\npublic class ConstructorOverloading {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Employee emp1 = new Employee();\n        Employee emp2 = new Employee(\"John\", 1001);\n        Employee emp3 = new Employee(\"Jane\", 1002, 50000.0);\n        Employee emp4 = new Employee(\"Bob\", 1003, 75000.0, \"IT\");\n        \n        System.out.println(emp1);  // Employee{name='Unknown', id=0, salary=0.00, dept='Unassigned'}\n        System.out.println(emp2);  // Employee{name='John', id=1001, salary=30000.00, dept='General'}\n        System.out.println(emp3);  // Employee{name='Jane', id=1002, salary=50000.00, dept='General'}\n        System.out.println(emp4);  // Employee{name='Bob', id=1003, salary=75000.00, dept='IT'}\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**4. Overloading with Type Promotion**:\n```java\nclass TypePromotion {\n    // Method that takes long\n    public void show(long value) {\n        System.out.println(\"Long method: \" + value);\n    }\n    \n    // Method that takes int\n    public void show(int value) {\n        System.out.println(\"Int method: \" + value);\n    }\n    \n    // Method that takes double\n    public void show(double value) {\n        System.out.println(\"Double method: \" + value);\n    }\n    \n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TypePromotion demo = new TypePromotion();\n        \n        byte b = 10;\n        short s = 20;\n        char c = 'A';\n        int i = 30;\n        long l = 40L;\n        float f = 50.5f;\n        double d = 60.6;\n        \n        demo.show(b);  // byte promoted to int -> calls show(int)\n        demo.show(s);  // short promoted to int -> calls show(int)\n        demo.show(c);  // char promoted to int -> calls show(int)\n        demo.show(i);  // exact match -> calls show(int)\n        demo.show(l);  // exact match -> calls show(long)\n        demo.show(f);  // float promoted to double -> calls show(double)\n        demo.show(d);  // exact match -> calls show(double)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**5. Ambiguous Overloading (Compile Error)**:\n```java\nclass AmbiguousOverload {\n    // These two methods can cause ambiguity\n    public void method(int a, double b) {\n        System.out.println(\"int, double\");\n    }\n    \n    public void method(double a, int b) {\n        System.out.println(\"double, int\");\n    }\n    \n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        AmbiguousOverload obj = new AmbiguousOverload();\n        \n        obj.method(10, 20.5);    // Clear: calls method(int, double)\n        obj.method(10.5, 20);    // Clear: calls method(double, int)\n        \n        // obj.method(10, 20);     // AMBIGUOUS - could call either method\n        // obj.method(10.5, 20.5); // AMBIGUOUS - both require type conversion\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**6. Real-World Example: Logger Class**:\n```java\nclass Logger {\n    public void log(String message) {\n        System.out.println(\"[INFO] \" + message);\n    }\n    \n    public void log(String level, String message) {\n        System.out.println(\"[\" + level + \"] \" + message);\n    }\n    \n    public void log(String level, String message, Exception e) {\n        System.out.println(\"[\" + level + \"] \" + message);\n        e.printStackTrace();\n    }\n    \n    public void log(int errorCode, String message) {\n        System.out.println(\"[ERROR-\" + errorCode + \"] \" + message);\n    }\n    \n    // Varargs for multiple messages\n    public void log(String... messages) {\n        System.out.println(\"[MULTI] \");\n        for (String msg : messages) {\n            System.out.println(\"  \" + msg);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\npublic class LoggerDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Logger logger = new Logger();\n        \n        logger.log(\"Application started\");\n        logger.log(\"WARN\", \"Low memory\");\n        logger.log(\"ERROR\", \"Database connection failed\", new RuntimeException(\"Connection timeout\"));\n        logger.log(404, \"Page not found\");\n        logger.log(\"Message 1\", \"Message 2\", \"Message 3\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Key Overloading Rules**:\n- **Same method name** required\n- **Different parameter signature** (type, count, or order)\n- **Return type doesn't matter** for overloading\n- **Access modifiers can vary**\n- **Compile-time resolution** (determined at compilation)\n- **Type promotion** affects method selection\n- **Ambiguous calls** cause compile errors",
