    category: "OOP Concepts",
    question: "What is method overriding?",
    idealAnswer: "**Method Overriding**: Child class provides specific implementation of method already defined in parent class.\n\n**1. Basic Method Overriding Example**:\n```java\n// Parent class\nclass Animal {\n    public void makeSound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal makes a sound\");\n    }\n    \n    public void eat() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal eats food\");\n    }\n    \n    // Final method cannot be overridden\n    public final void breathe() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal breathes air\");\n    }\n    \n    // Static method cannot be overridden (hidden instead)\n    public static void sleep() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal sleeps\");\n    }\n}\n\n// Child class overriding parent methods\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    public void makeSound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog barks: Woof!\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void eat() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog eats dog food\");\n    }\n    \n    // Cannot override final method\n    // @Override\n    // public void breathe() { }  // COMPILE ERROR\n    \n    // Hides static method (doesn't override)\n    public static void sleep() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog sleeps in kennel\");\n    }\n    \n    // New method specific to Dog\n    public void wagTail() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog wags tail happily\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Cat extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    public void makeSound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Cat meows: Meow!\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void eat() {\n        System.out.println(\"Cat eats cat food\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**2. Runtime Polymorphism with Overriding**:\n```java\npublic class OverridingDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Parent reference to child objects\n        Animal animal1 = new Dog();\n        Animal animal2 = new Cat();\n        Animal animal3 = new Animal();\n        \n        // Runtime polymorphism - method determined at runtime\n        animal1.makeSound();  // Dog barks: Woof!\n        animal2.makeSound();  // Cat meows: Meow!\n        animal3.makeSound();  // Animal makes a sound\n        \n        animal1.eat();       // Dog eats dog food\n        animal2.eat();       // Cat eats cat food\n        \n        // Final method always calls parent version\n        animal1.breathe();   // Animal breathes air\n        animal2.breathe();   // Animal breathes air\n        \n        // Static methods are called based on reference type\n        animal1.sleep();     // Animal sleeps (reference type Animal)\n        Dog.sleep();         // Dog sleeps in kennel (reference type Dog)\n        \n        // Type casting to access specific methods\n        if (animal1 instanceof Dog) {\n            Dog dog = (Dog) animal1;\n            dog.wagTail();    // Dog wags tail happily\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**3. Overriding Rules and Examples**:\n```java\nclass Parent {\n    // Different access modifiers\n    public void method1() {\n        System.out.println(\"Parent public method\");\n    }\n    \n    protected void method2() {\n        System.out.println(\"Parent protected method\");\n    }\n    \n    // Method with return type\n    public Number getValue() {\n        return 100;\n    }\n}\n\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    // Can make access modifier less restrictive\n    @Override\n    public void method1() {  // parent was public, child is public (same)\n        System.out.println(\"Child public method\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void method2() {  // parent was protected, child is public (less restrictive)\n        System.out.println(\"Child public method\");\n    }\n    \n    // Covariant return type (more specific)\n    @Override\n    public Integer getValue() {  // parent returns Number, child returns Integer\n        return 200;\n    }\n    \n    // Cannot make access modifier more restrictive\n    // @Override\n    // protected void method1() { }  // COMPILE ERROR\n}\n```\n\n**4. Using super to Call Parent Methods**:\n```java\nclass Vehicle {\n    public void start() {\n        System.out.println(\"Vehicle starting\");\n    }\n    \n    public void stop() {\n        System.out.println(\"Vehicle stopping\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Car extends Vehicle {\n    @Override\n    public void start() {\n        // Call parent method first\n        super.start();\n        System.out.println(\"Car engine started\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void stop() {\n        System.out.println(\"Car brakes applied\");\n        // Call parent method after\n        super.stop();\n    }\n    \n    public void demonstrateSuper() {\n        // Call specific parent method\n        super.start();  // Always calls Vehicle's start\n        this.start();   // Calls Car's start (overridden)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**5. Overriding with Exception Handling**:\n```java\nclass ParentClass {\n    public void method1() throws IOException {\n        System.out.println(\"Parent method with IOException\");\n    }\n    \n    public void method2() throws Exception {\n        System.out.println(\"Parent method with Exception\");\n    }\n    \n    public void method3() {\n        System.out.println(\"Parent method with no exceptions\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass ChildClass extends ParentClass {\n    // Can throw same exception or subclass\n    @Override\n    public void method1() throws FileNotFoundException {  // Subclass of IOException\n        System.out.println(\"Child method with FileNotFoundException\");\n    }\n    \n    // Can throw no exceptions\n    @Override\n    public void method2() {  // No exceptions (parent had Exception)\n        System.out.println(\"Child method with no exceptions\");\n    }\n    \n    // Cannot throw new or broader exceptions\n    @Override\n    public void method3() throws SQLException {  // COMPILE ERROR - parent had no exceptions\n        System.out.println(\"This won't compile\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**6. Abstract Methods and Overriding**:\n```java\n// Abstract class with abstract methods\nabstract class Shape {\n    abstract double area();\n    abstract double perimeter();\n    \n    // Concrete method\n    public void display() {\n        System.out.println(\"Shape with area: \" + area() + \", perimeter: \" + perimeter());\n    }\n}\n\nclass Circle extends Shape {\n    private double radius;\n    \n    public Circle(double radius) {\n        this.radius = radius;\n    }\n    \n    // Must implement abstract methods\n    @Override\n    public double area() {\n        return Math.PI * radius * radius;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public double perimeter() {\n        return 2 * Math.PI * radius;\n    }\n}\n\nclass Rectangle extends Shape {\n    private double width, height;\n    \n    public Rectangle(double width, double height) {\n        this.width = width;\n        this.height = height;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public double area() {\n        return width * height;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public double perimeter() {\n        return 2 * (width + height);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Key Overriding Rules**:\n- **Same method signature** (name and parameters)\n- **Inheritance relationship** required\n- **`@Override` annotation** recommended for compiler checking\n- **Access modifier** can't be more restrictive\n- **Return type** must be same or covariant (subclass)\n- **Exceptions** can't be broader than parent method\n- **`final` methods** cannot be overridden\n- **`static` methods** are hidden, not overridden\n- **`super` keyword** calls parent method implementation",
